o have such a term. The only way this can happen is if \u03b3=0. If \u03b3=0, then f(x)=\u03b2x\u00b2, and then:\n\nLHS: \u03b2(x\u00b2/y\u00b2 + 2x + y\u00b2).\n\nRHS: \u03b2x\u00b2/(\u03b2y\u00b2) + \u03b2y\u00b2 + \u03b1x = x\u00b2/y\u00b2 + \u03b2y\u00b2 + \u03b1x.\n\nSetting LHS=RHS:\n\n\u03b2x\u00b2/y\u00b2 + 2\u03b2x + \u03b2y\u00b2 = x\u00b2/y\u00b2 + \u03b2y\u00b2 + \u03b1x.\n\nComparing coefficients:\n\n\u03b2 =1 (from x\u00b2/y\u00b2 terms),\n\n2\u03b2 = \u03b1 (from x terms),\n\n\u03b2=\u03b2 (from y\u00b2 terms).\n\nTherefore, \u03b2=1 and \u03b1=2. So only when \u03b1=2, the function f(x)=x\u00b2 works.\n\nBut the problem states that \u03b1 is any positive rational. Therefore, for \u03b1\u22602, we need another form of f. \n\nLet\u2019s consider another approach. Suppose we set y = x. Then the equation becomes:\n\nf(x/x + x) = f(x)/f(x) + f(x) + \u03b1x \u2192 f(1 + x) = 1 + f(x) + \u03b1x, which is the same recursion as before. So we can\u2019t get new information from there.\n\nAlternatively, set x = ky for some k \u2208 Q+. Let\u2019s let x = ky, then:\n\nf( (ky)/y + y ) = f(ky)/f(y) + f(y) + \u03b1(ky).\n\nSimplify: f(k + y) = f(ky)/f(y) + f(y) + \u03b1ky.\n\nThis might be a more general form. If we can find a relation between f(k + y) and f(ky), maybe we can deduce something.\n\nAlternatively, let\u2019s consider the substitution z = x/y. Let z = x/y, so x = zy. Then the original equation becomes:\n\nf(z + y) = f(zy)/f(y) + f(y) + \u03b1(zy).\n\nThis is similar to Cauchy's equation but more complex. Let's see if we can assume a multiplicative function. Suppose f(zy) = f(z)f(y). If f is multiplicative, then f(zy)/f(y) = f(z). So the equation becomes f(z + y) = f(z) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y.\n\nThis resembles a modified Cauchy equation. The standard Cauchy equation f(z + y) = f(z) + f(y) is solved by linear functions, but here we have an additional term \u03b1 z y.\n\nSuppose we let f(z) = \u03b3 z\u00b2 + \u03b4 z. Let\u2019s try this. Then:\n\nf(z + y) = \u03b3(z + y)\u00b2 + \u03b4(z + y) = \u03b3(z\u00b2 + 2zy + y\u00b2) + \u03b4 z + \u03b4 y.\n\nThe RHS of the equation would be f(z) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y = \u03b3 z\u00b2 + \u03b4 z + \u03b3 y\u00b2 + \u03b4 y + \u03b1 z y.\n\nSet equal:\n\n\u03b3 z\u00b2 + 2\u03b3 z y + \u03b3 y\u00b2 + \u03b4 z + \u03b4 y = \u03b3 z\u00b2 + \u03b3 y\u00b2 + \u03b4 z + \u03b4 y + \u03b1 z y.\n\nCancel terms:\n\n2\u03b3 z y = \u03b1 z y \u21d2 2\u03b3 = \u03b1 \u21d2 \u03b3 = \u03b1/2.\n\nThus, if f(z) = (\u03b1/2) z\u00b2 + \u03b4 z, then the equation f(z + y) = f(z) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y holds. So this suggests that functions of the form f(z) = (\u03b1/2) z\u00b2 + \u03b4 z satisfy this modified Cauchy equation. Now, recall that we arrived at this under the assumption that f is multiplicative, i.e., f(zy) = f(z)f(y). Wait, no. Actually, we assumed that f is multiplicative to get f(zy)/f(y) = f(z), but then we also derived that f(z + y) = f(z) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y. But if we are not assuming f is multiplicative, but rather assuming a different form for f, then we might need to reconcile these two.\n\nWait, actually, in this step, we considered the substitution z = x/y, leading to f(z + y) = f(zy)/f(y) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y. Then, if we assume that f(zy)/f(y) is equal to some expression involving z and y, perhaps leading us to consider f(zy) = f(z)f(y), but that led us to a different equation. Alternatively, if f(zy) = f(z)f(y), then f(z + y) = f(z) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y. But even if f is multiplicative, the equation f(z + y) = f(z) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y must hold, which is only possible if \u03b1=0, but \u03b1 is positive. Therefore, multiplicative functions don't work here unless \u03b1=0.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps f is additive, but with an additional term. Suppose f(z) = \u03b3 z\u00b2 + \u03b4 z. Then we saw that f(z + y) = \u03b3(z + y)\u00b2 + \u03b4(z + y) = \u03b3 z\u00b2 + 2\u03b3 z y + \u03b3 y\u00b2 + \u03b4 z + \u03b4 y, and if we set this equal to f(z) + f(y) + \u03b1 z y = 