 increases from cos(1)\u22480.5403 to cos(0)=1.\n\nSimilarly, sin(sin\u03b8) is sin of a number in (0,1), which increases from sin(0)=0 to sin(1)\u22480.8415.\n\nSo we need to compare cos(cos\u03b8) and sin(sin\u03b8) over \u03b8 in (0, \u03c0/2). Let's see if cos(cos\u03b8) is always greater than sin(sin\u03b8).\n\nLet's check endpoints:\n\nAt \u03b8=0:\n\ncos(cos0)=cos(1)\u22480.5403\n\nsin(sin0)=sin(0)=0\n\nSo 0.5403 > 0, holds.\n\nAt \u03b8=\u03c0/2:\n\ncos(cos(\u03c0/2))=cos(0)=1\n\nsin(sin(\u03c0/2))=sin(1)\u22480.8415\n\nSo 1 > 0.8415, holds.\n\nNow check midpoints. Let's take \u03b8=\u03c0/4:\n\ncos(cos(\u03c0/4))=cos(\u221a2/2\u22480.7071)\u22480.7648\n\nsin(sin(\u03c0/4))=sin(\u221a2/2\u22480.7071)\u22480.6496\n\n0.7648 > 0.6496, holds.\n\nAnother point, say \u03b8 where cos\u03b8=0.5, which is \u03b8=\u03c0/3 (~1.047 radians), but wait \u03b8 is in (0, \u03c0/2). So cos\u03b8=0.5 corresponds to \u03b8=\u03c0/3, which is 60 degrees, but \u03c0/3\u22481.047, which is greater than \u03c0/2\u22481.5708. Wait, no: \u03b8=\u03c0/3 is 60 degrees, which is less than 90 degrees (\u03c0/2). Wait, cos(\u03c0/3)=0.5. So \u03b8=\u03c0/3 is in (0, \u03c0/2).\n\nCompute cos(cos\u03b8)=cos(0.5)\u22480.8776\n\nsin(sin\u03b8)=sin(sin(\u03c0/3))=sin(\u221a3/2\u22480.8660)\u22480.7616\n\nSo 0.8776 >0.7616, holds.\n\nAnother point, \u03b8 where sin\u03b8=0.5, which is \u03b8=\u03c0/6 (~0.523 radians):\n\ncos(cos\u03b8)=cos(cos(\u03c0/6))=cos(\u221a3/2\u22480.8660)\u22480.6456\n\nsin(sin\u03b8)=sin(0.5)\u22480.4794\n\n0.6456 >0.4794, holds.\n\nSo far, all points checked satisfy cos(cos\u03b8) > sin(sin\u03b8). Let's check if there could be any \u03b8 where this inequality reverses.\n\nConsider the function g(\u03b8)=cos(cos\u03b8) - sin(sin\u03b8). We need to check if this is always positive.\n\nCompute derivative g'(\u03b8):\n\ng'(\u03b8)= -sin(cos\u03b8)*(-sin\u03b8) - cos(sin\u03b8)*cos\u03b8\n\n= sin\u03b8 * sin(cos\u03b8) - cos\u03b8 * cos(sin\u03b8)\n\nNot sure about the