os\u03b8) is increasing from 0.5403 to 1, and sin(sin\u03b8) is increasing from 0 to 0.8415. So we need to check if the increasing function cos(cos\u03b8) stays above the increasing function sin(sin\u03b8). Since at \u03b8=0, cos(cos\u03b8)=0.5403 >0=sin(sin\u03b8). At \u03b8 approaching \u03c0/2, cos(cos\u03b8) approaches 1 >0.8415\u2248sin(sin\u03b8). So if both are increasing, but which one increases faster?\n\nWait, maybe there is a crossing point where cos(cos\u03b8) equals sin(sin\u03b8). Let's check if such a \u03b8 exists.\n\nSet cos(cos\u03b8) = sin(sin\u03b8). Let's look for a solution in (0, \u03c0/2).\n\nLet\u2019s define h(\u03b8)=cos(cos\u03b8) - sin(sin\u03b8). We need to see if h(\u03b8)=0 for any \u03b8 in (0, \u03c0/2).\n\nWe know h(0)=cos(1)\u22480.5403 >0 and h(\u03c0/2)=1 - sin(1)\u22480.1585 >0. If h(\u03b8) ever dips below zero in between, then there's a crossing. Otherwise, it stays positive.\n\nTo check, let's compute h(\u03b8) at some points.\n\nAt \u03b8=\u03c0/4, we had h(\u03c0/4)=cos(cos(\u03c0/4)) - sin(sin(\u03c0/4))\u22480.7648 -0.6496\u22480.1152>0.\n\nAt \u03b8=1 radian (~57.3 degrees):\n\ncos(cos1)=cos(0.5403)\u22480.8575\n\nsin(sin1)=sin(0.8415)\u22480.7431\n\nThus, h(1)=0.8575 -0.7431\u22480.1144>0.\n\nAt \u03b8=0. Let's pick \u03b8=0. Let's try \u03b8=0. Let's go back. Wait, we already checked \u03b8=0, \u03c0/4, \u03c0/2, etc.\n\nAlternatively, check \u03b8 where sin(sin\u03b8) is around 0.8575. Wait, sin(sin\u03b8)=0.8575. Then sin\u03b8=arcsin(0.8575)\u22481.029 radians. But sin\u03b8 can't exceed 1, so arcsin(0.8575)\u22481.029>\u03c0/2\u22481.5708? Wait, no. Wait, arcsin(0.8575) is the angle whose sine is 0.8575, which is approximately 1.029 radians (~59 degrees). But sin\u03b8 can only be up to 1, so \u03b8 would be arcsin(arcsin(0.8575))? Wait, this is getting complicated.\n\nAlternatively, check \u03b8 where sin(sin\u03b8)=cos(cos\u03b8). Let\u2019s try \u03b8=0.8 radians (~45.8 degrees):\n\nsin(sin0.8)=sin(0.7174)\u224