) = c\u00b2(a\u00b2 - b\u00b2)\n\nIf a\u00b2 \u2260 b\u00b2, we can divide both sides by (a\u00b2 - b\u00b2):\n\n2(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) = c\u00b2\n\nBut from earlier, we also had 2c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2. Wait, substituting c\u00b2 from here into the other equation.\n\nFrom 2c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2, so c\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/2.\n\nBut from the equation above, if a\u00b2 \u2260 b\u00b2, then 2(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) = c\u00b2. Therefore:\n\nc\u00b2 = 2(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nBut from 2c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2, c\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/2.\n\nSo we have two expressions for c\u00b2:\n\nFrom 2c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2: c\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/2\n\nFrom subtracting the equations (assuming a\u00b2 \u2260 b\u00b2): c\u00b2 = 2(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nTherefore:\n\n(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/2 = 2(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\na\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = 4(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nSubtract a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 from both sides:\n\n0 = 3(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nWhich implies a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = 0. But since a and b are sides of a triangle, they must be positive, so this is impossible. Therefore, our assumption that a\u00b2 \u2260 b\u00b2 leads to a contradiction. Therefore, we must have a\u00b2 = b\u00b2, i.e., a = b.\n\nSo, a = b. Then, since we have from the earlier relation 2c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2, substituting a = b gives 2c\u00b2 = 2a\u00b2 => c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 => c = a. Therefore, a = b = c. So the triangle is equilateral. Therefore, the only solution is an equilateral triangle. So answer is (B).\n\nWait, but let me check if the equations could be satisfied by another triangle. Suppose it's a scalene triangle. Then all sides are different. But according to the above, the only solution is equilateral. So unless there's a miscalculation in my steps.\n\nLet me recap:\n\n1. Subtract the two given equations to get 2c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2.\n\n2. Substitute back into first equation and manipulate to find c = b.\n\n3. Then from 2c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + 