of blondes (knights) and L = 200 - B as the number of brunettes (liars).\n\nNow, each blonde (knight) will truthfully say \"You are a liar\" to brunettes (who are liars) and \"You are a blonde\" to other blondes (who are knights and blondes).\n\nEach brunette (liar) will lie when speaking to others. So when a brunette (liar) speaks to a blonde (knight), the truth is \"You are a knight\" and \"You are a blonde,\" so the liar must say the opposite. But they can only choose between \"You are a liar\" or \"You are a blonde.\" The truth is the listener is a blonde knight, so \"You are a blonde\" is true, and \"You are a liar\" is false. Therefore, the liar must say \"You are a liar\" to lie.\n\nWhen a brunette (liar) speaks to another brunette (liar), the truth is \"You are a liar\" and \"You are a brunette.\" The liar must lie. The truth is both statements \"You are a liar\" and \"You are a brunette\" are true, so the liar must say the opposite. But they can only choose between \"You are a liar\" or \"You are a blonde.\" Therefore, they must lie about the hair color and say \"You are a blonde\" (since \"You are a brunette\" is true, the lie would be \"You are a blonde\").\n\nTherefore, summarizing:\n\n- Blondes (knights) say \"You are a liar\" to brunettes (liars) and \"You are a blonde\" to other blondes.\n- Brunettes (liars) say \"You are a liar\" to blondes (knights) and \"You are a blonde\" to other brunettes.\n\nNow, let's compute the total number of \"You are a liar\" statements.\n\nFirst, consider statements from blondes:\n\nEach blonde says \"You are a liar\" to each brunette. There are B blondes, each saying \"You are a liar\" to L brunettes. So that's B * L \"You are a liar\" statements.\n\nEach blonde also says \"You are a blonde\" to other blondes. There are B blondes, each saying \"You are a blonde\" to (B - 1) blondes. But we're focusing on \"You are a liar\" statements, so these don't contribute.\n\nNow, consider statements from brunettes:\n\nEach brunette says \"You are a liar\" to each blonde. There are L brunettes, each saying \"You are a liar\" to B blondes. So that's L * B \"You are a liar\" statements.\n\nEach brunette says \"You are a blonde\" to other brunettes. There are L brunettes, each saying \"You are a blonde\" to (L - 1) brunettes. These are \"You are a blonde\" statements.\n\nTherefore, total \"You are a liar\" statements = B*L + L*B = 2BL.\n\nTotal statements = 200*199 = 39,800.\n\nWe are told that \"You are a liar\" was said more than half the time, i.e., more than 19,900 times. Therefore:\n\n2BL > 19,900\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\nBL > 9,950\n\nSo the product of the number of blondes and brunettes must be greater than 9,950.\n\nBut B + L = 200, so L = 200 - B. Therefore:\n\nB*(200 - B) > 9,950\n\nLet's write this inequality:\n\n200B - B\u00b2 > 9,950\n\nRearranging:\n\n-B\u00b2 + 200B - 9,950 > 0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1 (which reverses the inequality):\n\nB\u00b2 - 200B + 9,950 < 0\n\nWe need to find the values of B for which this quadratic is negative.\n\nFirst, find the roots of the equation B\u00b2 - 200B + 9,950 = 0.\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\n\nB = [200 \u00b1 sqrt(200\u00b2 - 4*1*9,950)] / 2\n\nCalculate discriminant:\n\nD = 40,000 - 39,800 = 200\n\nTherefore:\n\nB = [200 \u00b1 sqrt(200)] / 2\n\nsqrt(200) = 10*sqrt(2) \u2248 14.1421\n\nTherefore:\n\nB \u2248 [200 \u00b1 14.1421]/2 \u2248 [200 + 14.1421]/2 \u2248 214.1421/2 \u2248 107.071\n\nOr B \u2248 [200 - 14.1421]/2 \u2248 185.8579/2 \u2248 92.9289\n\nSo the quadratic is negative between the roots \u224892.9289 and \u2248107.071.\n\nSince B must be an integer (number of people), the values of B that satisfy the inequality are integers from 93 to 107 inclusive.\n\nBut wait, we need to check if B can actually take these values. Remember that B is the number of blondes (knights) and L = 200 - B is the number of brunettes (liars). Since both B and L must be non-negative integers, B can range from 0 to 200. However, our earlier analysis concluded that all knights are blondes and all liars are brunettes, which means that B must equal the number of knights, and L the number of liars.\n\nBut there's no restriction on the number of knights and liars except that they must be non-negative integers. However, from the problem statement, it's possible to have any combination of knights and liars as long as blondes=knights and brunettes=liars.\n\nHowever, we derived that BL > 9,950, leading to B in (92.9289, 107.071), so integer B from 93 to 107 inclusive. But we need to ensure that the counts make sense.\n\nWait a second, but if B is 93, then L is 107, and BL = 93*107=9,951, which is just over 9,950. Similarly, B=107 gives L=93, BL=107*93=9,951. So these are the boundary cases. Then, for B from 93 to 107 inclusive, BL >=9,951 which is >9,950, satisfying the inequality.\n\nHowever, wait, the inequality is BL > 9,950. So BL must be at least 9,951. Let's check B=93 and L=107:\n\n93*107 = 93*(100 + 7) = 9300 + 651 = 9951. Yes, exactly 9,951. Similarly, B=107, L=93: same product.\n\nTherefore, B can range from 93 to 107 inclusive, giving BL >=9,951, which satisfies BL >9,950.\n\nHowever, we must also ensure that the counts of blondes and brunettes make sense in terms of the statements made. Recall that we concluded that all blondes are knights and all brunettes are liars. Therefore, the number of blondes B must be equal to the number of knights, and brunettes L=200-B must be the number of liars.\n\nBut there's no additional constraint here, so as long as B is between 93 and 107 inclusive, the total number of \"You are a liar\" statements would be 2BL, which would be greater than 19,900, satisfying the problem's condition.\n\nHowever, the problem asks for \"What number of blondes could there be on the island? Find all possible options and prove there are no others.\"\n\nWait, but according to our analysis, B can be any integer from 93 to 107 inclusive. However, we need to confirm if all these values are