x of the left quadratic is at (-5,-3), and it opens upwards. So, the left quadratic is f(x)= (x +5)^2 -3 for x in [-6,-3], which is indeed an upward opening parabola. That makes sense because when you reflect the right quadratic, which opens downward, over the origin, it becomes upward opening.\n\nTherefore, combining all pieces, the function is:\n\n- For x in [-6,-3]: f(x) = (x +5)^2 -3\n- For x in [-3,3]: f(x) = -x/3\n- For x in [3,6]: f(x) = - (x -5)^2 +3\n\nThis covers the entire interval [-6,6].\n\nLet me verify the continuity at x=3 and x=-3.\n\nAt x=3: from the right, f(3) = - (3 -5)^2 +3 = -4 +3 = -1. From the left, f(3) = -3/3 = -1. So continuous at x=3.\n\nAt x=-3: from the left (x approaching -3 from the left), f(-3) = (-3 +5)^2 -3 = 4 -3=1. From the right (x approaching -3 from the right in [-3,3]), f(-3) = -(-3)/3=1. So continuous at x=-3.\n\nAlso, check the other points. At x=6: f(6)= - (6-5)^2 +3= -1 +3=2, which matches given. At x=-6: f(-6)= (-6 +5)^2 -3=1 -3=-2, which is -f(6)=-2, correct.\n\nAdditionally, check the maximum at x=5: f(5)=3, which is the highest point on [3,6], and since the coefficient is negative, it's the maximum. Similarly, on [-6,-3], the minimum would be at x=-5, f(-5)=-3, which is the lowest point on that interval, as the parabola opens upwards.\n\nTherefore, all conditions are satisfied: the function is odd, linear on [0,3], quadratic on [3,6], with f(5)=3 and f(6)=2.\n\nSo, the analytical expression is as above. To write it properly:\n\nFor x \u2208 [0,3], f(x) = - (1/3)x\n\nFor x \u2208 [3,6], f(x) = - (x -5)\u00b2 +3\n\nFor x \u2208 [-3,0), f(x) = - (1/3)x\n\nFor x \u2208 [-6,-3), f(x) = (x +5)\u00b2 -3\n\nAlternatively, since the function is odd, we can express the negative x parts in terms of the positive x parts, but the problem probably expects the full piecewise expression.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is written as a piecewise function with four intervals, as above.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe analytical expression of 