operty of odd functions: \\( f(x) = -f(-x) \\).\n\n2. On the interval \\([0, 3]\\), \\( f(x) \\) is a linear function. Let:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = kx \\quad \\text{for} \\quad 0 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 3\n   \\]\n\n3. On the interval \\([3, 6]\\), \\( f(x) \\) is a quadratic function. Given \\( f(5) = 3 \\) (maximum value) and \\( f(6) = 2 \\), we can assume:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = a(x - 5)^2 + 3 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad 3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 6\n   \\]\n\n4. To find \\( a \\), use \\( f(6) = 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   f(6) = a(6 - 5)^2 + 3 = 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a(1)^2 + 3 = 2 \\implies a + 3 = 2 \\implies a = -1\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = -(x - 5)^2 + 3 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad 3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 6\n   \\]\n\n5. Determine \\( f(3) \\):\n   \\[\n   f(3) = -(3 - 5)^2 + 3 = -(2)^2 + 3 = -4 + 3 = -1\n   \\]\n\n6. Verify \\( f(x) \\) for \\( 0 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   f(x) = kx \\quad \\text{and} \\quad f(3) = 3k = -1\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( k \\):\n   \\[\n   3k = -1 \\implies k = -\\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = -\\frac{1}{3} x \\quad \\text{for} \\quad 0 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 3\n   \\]\n\n7. Utilizing the property of odd functions \\( f(x) = -f(-x) \\):\n   - For \\( -6 \\leqslant x \\leqslant -3 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(x) = -f(-x) = -[-(x+5)^2 + 3] = (x+5)^2 - 3\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\( -3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 0 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(x) = -f(-x) = -\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}(-x)\\right) = -\\left(-\\frac{1}{3} x\\right) = -\\frac{1}{3} x\n     \\]\n\n8. Summarizing all intervals, the analytic expression for \\( f(x) \\) is:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = \n   \\begin{cases}\n   (x+5)^2 - 3 & \\text{for} \\; -6 \\leqslant x < -3 \\\\\n   -\\frac{1}{3} x & \\text{for} \\; -3 \\leqslant x < 3 \\\\\n   -(x - 5)^2 + 3 & \\text{for} \\; 3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 6 \n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nf(x) = \n\\begin{cases}\n(x+5)^2 - 3 & \\text{for} \\; -6 \\leqslant x < -3 \\\\\n-\\frac{1}{3} x & \\text{for} \\; -3 \\leqslant x < 3 \\\\\n-(x - 5)^2 + 3 & \\text{for} \\; 3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 6 \n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Natural numbers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are such that \\( a^{n+1} + b^{n+1} \\) is divisible by \\( a^n + b^n \\) for infinitely many distinct natural numbers \\( n \\). Must it be the case that \\( a = b \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem here: if natural numbers a and b are such that a^(n+1) + b^(n+1) is divisible by a^n + b^n for infinitely many distinct natural numbers n, does that necessarily mean a equals b? Hmm, interesting. Let me try to figure this out step by step.\n\nFirst, let's parse the problem. We have two natural numbers, a and b. The condition is that for infinitely many natural numbers n, a^(n+1) + b^(n+1) is divisible by a^n + b^n. The question is whether this forces a and b to be equal. So, we need to show that a = b is necessary under these conditions, or find a counterexample where a \u2260 b but the divisibility still holds for infinitely many n.\n\nLet me start by considering some small examples. Maybe if a and b are equal, then the divisibility condition holds. Let's check that. If a = b, then a^n + b^n = 2a^n. Then a^(n+1) + b^(n+1) = 2a^(n+1). So, 2a^(n+1) divided by 2a^n is a, which is an integer. Therefore, if a = b, then the divisibility holds for all n. So, certainly, if a = b, the condition is satisfied for all n, hence infinitely many n. So that direction is clear.\n\nBut the problem is asking the converse: if the divisibility holds for infinitely many n, must a = b? So, suppose a \u2260 b. Can we have infinitely many n such that a^(n+1) + b^(n+1) is divisible by a^n + b^n? If not, then the answer is yes, a must equal b.\n\nLet me think about how to approach this. Maybe we can look at the ratio [a^(n+1) + b^(n+1)] / [a^n + b^n] and see under what conditions this is an integer. Let's compute this rati