\\} \\) and \\( N = \\left\\{ x \\mid x = \\pm \\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi, \\, k \\in \\mathbf{Z} \\right\\} \\), we need to determine their relationship.\n\n1. **Set M**: This set consists of all angles of the form \\( \\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\) or \\( \\frac{5}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\) for any integer \\( k \\). These angles are co-terminal with \\( \\frac{7}{6}\\pi \\) or \\( \\frac{5}{6}\\pi \\).\n\n2. **Set N**: This set consists of all angles of the form \\( \\pm \\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\) for any integer \\( k \\). The negative angle \\( -\\frac{7}{6}\\pi \\) can be rewritten as \\( \\frac{5}{6}\\pi \\) by adding \\( 2\\pi \\), since \\( -\\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2\\pi = \\frac{5}{6}\\pi \\).\n\n3. **Equivalence Check**:\n   - For any element \\( x \\) in \\( N \\), it can be written as \\( \\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\) or \\( -\\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\). The term \\( -\\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\) is equivalent to \\( \\frac{5}{6}\\pi + 2(k-1)\\pi \\), which is an element of \\( M \\).\n   - Conversely, for any element \\( x \\) in \\( M \\), it can be written as \\( \\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\) (which is in \\( N \\)) or \\( \\frac{5}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\). The term \\( \\frac{5}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\) is equivalent to \\( -\\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2(k+1)\\pi \\), which is in \\( N \\).\n\nThus, every element of \\( M \\) is in \\( N \\) and every element of \\( N \\) is in \\( M \\), proving that \\( M \\) and \\( N \\) are equal.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "To determine the relationship between the sets \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), we will analyze the elements of each set in detail.\n\n#### Step 1: Express Set \\(M\\)\nSet \\(M\\) is given by \n\\[ \nM = \\left\\{ x \\left| x = \\frac{7}{6} \\pi + 2k\\pi \\, \\text{ or } \\, x = \\frac{5}{6} \\pi + 2k\\pi, \\, k \\in \\mathbf{Z} \\right. \\right\\}\n\\]\n\nThis means that the elements of set \\(M\\) can be written in the form:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7}{6}\\pi + 2k\\pi \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = \\frac{5}{6