 between them.\n\nFrom (2,2) to (14,18):\n\nCompute 14/2=7, 18/2=9. Not a direct multiple. Let's see if there's a linear relation.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if (14,18) can be obtained by multiplying (2,2) by some matrix or transformation.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we have solutions (a1, b1) and (a2, b2). Then, perhaps:\n\n5(a1a2)^2 -3(b1b2)^2 =?\n\nNot sure. Alternatively, maybe use the composition of solutions. Let me check:\n\nSuppose we have two solutions (a1, b1) and (a2, b2). Let's try to compose them as follows:\n\n( sqrt(5)a1 + sqrt(3)b1 )*( sqrt(5)a2 + sqrt(3)b2 ) = sqrt(5*5)a1a2 + sqrt(15)(a1b2 + a2b1) + sqrt(3*3)b1b2\n\n=5a1a2 + 3b1b2 + sqrt(15)(a1b2 + a2b1)\n\nBut this might not directly relate to another solution. Alternatively, maybe look for a recurrence relation based on the minimal solution.\n\nAlternatively, note that the solutions (2,2) and (14,18). Let's see if there's a multiplier here.\n\nCompute 14/2=7, 18/2=9. So perhaps a multiplier of 7 and 9? Let's check if 7 and 9 relate to coefficients 5 and 3. Maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the next solution can be found via:\n\na' = 7a + 6b\n\nb' = 10a + 7b\n\nLet's test this with (a=2, b=2):\n\na' =7*2 +6*2=14 +12=26\n\nb'=10*2 +7*2=20 +14=34\n\nThen check 5*(26)^2 -3*(34)^2=5*676 -3*1156=3380 -3468= -88 \u22608. Not valid.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps another multiplier. Let's suppose that:\n\nGiven a solution (a, b), the next solution is (7a + 6b, 10a + 7b). Wait, with (2,2):\n\na'=7*2 +6*2=14 +12=26, b'=10*2 +7*2=20 +14=34. As before, gives -88. Not working.\n\nAlternatively, maybe (7a + 6b, 10a + 9b). Let's try:\n\na'=7*2 +6*2=26, b'=10*2 +9*2=38. Then 5*26\u00b2 -3*38\u00b2=5*676 -3*1444=3380 -4332= -952. Not 8.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another approach. Let's see the ratio between a and b in the two solutions:\n\nFirst solution: a=2, b=2. So a/b=1.\n\nSecond solution: a=14, b=18. a/b\u22480.777...\n\nNot obvious.\n\nAlternatively, compute the difference between the solutions. From (2,2) to (1