ite N=8n(17n +3).\n\nGiven that n= (a\u00b2 -1)/3, and 17n +3=(17a\u00b2 -8)/3.\n\nTherefore, N=8*((a\u00b2 -1)/3)*((17a\u00b2 -8)/3)=8*(a\u00b2 -1)(17a\u00b2 -8)/9.\n\nLet me expand the numerator:\n\n(a\u00b2 -1)(17a\u00b2 -8)=17a^4 -8a\u00b2 -17a\u00b2 +8=17a^4 -25a\u00b2 +8.\n\nSo N=8*(17a^4 -25a\u00b2 +8)/9.\n\nBut this may not help directly. However, since a\u00b2=3n +1, substitute a\u00b2:\n\nN=8*(17(3n +1)^2 -25(3n +1) +8)/9\n\n=8*(17(9n\u00b2 +6n +1) -75n -25 +8)/9\n\n=8*(153n\u00b2 +102n +17 -75n -25 +8)/9\n\n=8*(153n\u00b2 +27n)/9\n\n=8*9*(17n\u00b2 +3n)/9\n\n=8*(17n\u00b2 +3n). Which brings us back to the original expression. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, consider that since n is such that 3n +1 and5n -1 are squares, perhaps there's a relation that allows N to be expressed as a sum of two squares. Alternatively, note that the product of two sums of squares is a sum of squares, and the product of sums of squares is a sum of squares.\n\nGiven that 3n +1=a\u00b2 and5n -1=b\u00b2, then perhaps we can relate N to a and b.\n\nLet me try to express N=8(17n\u00b2 +3n) in terms of a and b.\n\nFirst, note that:\n\nFrom 3n +1 =a\u00b2 \u21923n=a\u00b2 -1\n\nFrom5n -1 =b\u00b2 \u21925n =b\u00b2 +1\n\nLet me solve for n in both:\n\nFrom first: n=(a\u00b2 -1)/3\n\nFrom second: n=(b\u00b2 +1)/5\n\nMultiply these two expressions:\n\nn\u00b2= (a\u00b2 -1)(b\u00b2 +1)/15\n\nMultiply both sides by15:\n\n15n\u00b2=(a\u00b2 -1)(b\u00b2 +1)\n\nExpand RHS:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2 -1\n\nThus, 15n\u00b2= a\u00b2b\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2 -1\n\nBut this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 17n\u00b2 +3n:\n\n=17n\u00b2 +3n=17n\u00b2 +3n\n\nBut from above, 3n=a\u00b2 -1, so substitute:\n\n=17n\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -1\n\nBut 17n\u00b2=17n\u00b2. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express in terms of a and b:\n\nWe have a\u00b2=3n +1 and b\u00b2=5n -1.\n\nMultiply these:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2=(3n +1)(5n -1)=15n\u00b2 -3n +5n -1=15n\u00b2 +2n -1.\n\nTherefore, a\u00b2b\u0