n\n1. Determine the equation of the ellipse \\( C \\).\n2. A line \\( l \\) passing through point \\( P(4,1) \\) intersects the ellipse \\( C \\) at two distinct points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\). Let \\( Q \\) be a point on segment \\( AB \\) satisfying \\( |\\overrightarrow{AP}| \\cdot |\\overrightarrow{QB}| = |\\overrightarrow{AQ}| \\cdot |\\overrightarrow{PB}| \\). Find the locus of point \\( Q \\).\n\n\n**(1) Determine the equation of the ellipse \\( C \\):**\n\n1. **Identify the foci of the ellipse:**\n   The left focus \\( F_{1} \\) is given as \\( (-\\sqrt{2}, 0) \\) and the right focus \\( F_{2} \\) is \\( (\\sqrt{2}, 0) \\). Hence, the distance between the foci is \\( 2c \\) where \\( c = \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\n2. **Calculate the semi-major axis \\( a \\):**\n   Recall that an ellipse satisfies the condition that for any point \\( M (\\sqrt{2}, 1) \\) on the ellipse, the sum of the distances to the foci is equal to \\( 2a \\):\n\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{|MF_1| + |MF_2|}{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Compute the distances from \\( M \\) to \\( F_{1} \\) and \\( F_{2} \\):**\n   \n   \\[\n   |MF_{1}| = \\sqrt{( \\sqrt{2} - (-\\sqrt{2}))^2 + (1 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 1} = \\sqrt{5}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   |MF_{2}| = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{2})^2 + (1 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 1} = 1\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find \\( a \\):**\n   \n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{ \\sqrt{5} + 1 }{ 2 } = 2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate \\( b^{2} \\):**\n   Using the relationship \\( b^2 = a^2 - c^2 \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   b^2 = 4 - 2 = 2 \\quad \\text{therefore} \\quad b = \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Write the equation of the ellipse:**\n   \n   \\[\n   C : \\frac{x^2} { 4 } + \\frac{ y^2 } { 2 } = 1 \n   \\]\n   Hence, the equation of the ellipse is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{C : \\frac{x^2}{4} + \\frac{y^2}{2} = 1}\n   \\]\n\n**(2) Find the locus of point \\( Q \\):**\n\n1. **Determine the equation of the line \\( l \\) passing through \\( P(4,1) \\):**\n   \n   Let the equation of the line \\( l \\) be:\n   \n   \\[\n   y - 1 = k (x 